const articleHtml = "<p>Biosecurity is the set of practices that reduce the chance of a pathogen entering a bird collection and spreading once it is there. Testing is useful, but it is not the whole system.</p>\n<p>A collection can have a perfect test report and still have a biosecurity problem if new birds share airspace, equipment moves between rooms, or visitors handle birds without washing their hands.</p>\n<h2>What Is Avian Biosecurity?</h2>\n<p>Avian biosecurity is a management plan built around three questions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>How do we keep pathogens out?</li>\n<li>How do we stop them from spreading inside?</li>\n<li>How do we know whether the plan is working?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The answers include physical separation, traffic control, cleaning, health records, and targeted testing.</p>\n<h2>The Three Core Components</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>What it covers</th>\n<th>Practical examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Isolation</td>\n<td>Keeping new, sick, or suspect birds away from the main group</td>\n<td>Separate room, separate airspace, dedicated equipment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Traffic control</td>\n<td>Controlling who and what moves between areas</td>\n<td>Footwear changes, hand hygiene, visitor limits, equipment logs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sanitation</td>\n<td>Reducing contamination on surfaces, tools, and hands</td>\n<td>Cleaning, disinfection, drying, waste management</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Testing supports these layers. It does not replace them.</p>\n<h2>How Disease Testing Supports Biosecurity</h2>\n<p>Testing can:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>identify a pathogen in a new bird before introduction;</li>\n<li>establish a baseline for a group;</li>\n<li>help investigate an outbreak;</li>\n<li>support a decision to isolate or retest;</li>\n<li>provide a record of health status over time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Testing cannot:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>prove a bird is free of every pathogen;</li>\n<li>remove contamination from a surface;</li>\n<li>replace quarantine;</li>\n<li>decide treatment for a veterinarian.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Managing New and Returning Birds</h2>\n<p>New arrivals and birds returning from shows, races, or sales should be treated as separate risk events.</p>\n<p>A workable process:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>assess the source and travel history;</li>\n<li>examine the bird;</li>\n<li>decide whether targeted testing is warranted;</li>\n<li>quarantine in a separate area;</li>\n<li>monitor for signs;</li>\n<li>review the results and veterinary advice before introduction.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A negative test from the seller is context, not a clearance.</p>\n<h2>Separating Healthy, Quarantined and Sick Birds</h2>\n<p>A simple three-zone model helps:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Main collection:</strong> healthy, stable birds.</li>\n<li><strong>Quarantine:</strong> new or returning birds with no current signs.</li>\n<li><strong>Isolation:</strong> birds with signs or a positive result that requires separation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The zones should have separate equipment and a defined traffic order. Handle the main collection first, then quarantine, then isolation, or follow the veterinarian's advice.</p>\n<h2>Cleaning and Disinfection</h2>\n<p>Cleaning removes organic material; disinfection reduces viable pathogens. A surface that is not cleaned first may not be effectively disinfected.</p>\n<p>Key practices:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>remove droppings, feed, and bedding before disinfection;</li>\n<li>use a disinfectant appropriate for the surface and pathogen;</li>\n<li>allow the correct contact time;</li>\n<li>clean water containers and utensils separately;</li>\n<li>dry surfaces where possible;</li>\n<li>discard or disinfect porous materials that cannot be cleaned.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Personnel, Equipment and Visitor Management</h2>\n<p>People and equipment move pathogens as easily as birds.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep dedicated clothing or footwear for each area.</li>\n<li>Wash hands before and after handling birds.</li>\n<li>Clean and disinfect shared tools between groups.</li>\n<li>Limit visitors and record who enters the bird areas.</li>\n<li>Do not let visitors handle birds without a hygiene protocol.</li>\n<li>Keep food, medication, and clean supplies away from dirty areas.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Maintaining Bird Health Records</h2>\n<p>A record system does not need to be complicated. It needs to be consistent.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Record</th>\n<th>Why it matters</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bird ID, species, origin</td>\n<td>Identifies the individual and its exposure history</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Arrival and quarantine dates</td>\n<td>Shows separation and timing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Veterinary examinations</td>\n<td>Records clinical findings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tests, samples, results</td>\n<td>Tracks the evidence trail</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Treatments or interventions</td>\n<td>Provides context for later results</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Movements between groups</td>\n<td>Helps trace contacts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cleaning and disinfection log</td>\n<td>Shows whether the plan was followed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Records are especially useful when a result is unexpected or when a new bird's history is unclear.</p>\n<h2>Creating a Monitoring System</h2>\n<p>A monitoring system should include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a schedule of observations and tests;</li>\n<li>a named person responsible for each task;</li>\n<li>a defined response to abnormal signs;</li>\n<li>a review of results over time;</li>\n<li>a plan for updating the protocol.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The system should be practical enough to follow on a busy day. A protocol that only works when nothing else is happening is not a biosecurity system.</p>\n<h2>Reviewing Biosecurity Performance</h2>\n<p>Review the plan after:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a new bird introduction;</li>\n<li>a positive result;</li>\n<li>an outbreak;</li>\n<li>a change in housing or staff;</li>\n<li>a show or race season.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Ask what worked, what was skipped, and what should change. A biosecurity plan is a living document, not a certificate on the wall.</p>\n<h2>Scenario: Eight show birds return to a 60-bird collection</h2>\n<p>Eight pigeons return to a 60-bird loft after a show, and one develops a respiratory sign two days later. A screening sample is positive for pigeon herpesvirus. The loft uses shared crates, a common water line, and one preparation table, and the returnees mixed with the home team at feeding before the result. The uncertainty is whether the positive reflects reactivation, recent exposure, or spread; one sample does not map the loft.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Separate the affected bird and seven other returnees from the main loft. Use dedicated crates and equipment, and handle the main team first.</li>\n<li>Confirm the result and sample details with the veterinarian and review clinical signs. Trace show contacts, transport, feeding equipment, and people moving between groups.</li>\n<li>Prioritize birds with signs or direct exposure before broader sampling. The veterinarian, laboratory, and official reporting rules determine next samples.</li>\n<li>Clean and disinfect crates, tables, and water equipment according to product labels. Remove organic material first; a positive test does not identify contaminated surfaces.</li>\n<li>Keep movement and show entries suspended until the veterinarian reviews the evidence. A negative contact test does not prove freedom, and one positive does not prove the whole loft is affected.</li>\n</ol>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr><th>Result</th><th>What changes</th><th>What it does not prove</th></tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Index bird Detected</td><td>Isolate, obtain veterinary review, and trace shared equipment and contacts.</td><td>The whole loft is infected or the source and timing of exposure.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Direct contact Not Detected</td><td>Continue observation and separation; retest if advised.</td><td>The contact is free of infection or safe to return.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Contact Detected</td><td>Expand separation and record the contact network for veterinary review.</td><td>Clinical disease or that every bird in the loft is affected.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>What we would do:</strong> We would treat the positive as a reason to tighten separation, trace shared equipment and transport, and document every movement, not as proof that the whole loft is infected. Our <a href=\"/bird-health-screening-biosecurity/\">testing, isolation, and record-keeping framework</a> makes those steps reviewable. The <a href=\"/bird-quarantine-testing-protocol/\">quarantine testing protocol</a> supports keeping returnees separate during veterinary follow-up. This advice would not apply if several birds were ill or a reportable disease were suspected; official guidance would come first.</p>\n<p>This is a testing-strategy example, not a diagnosis or treatment plan, and a veterinarian must interpret results with clinical findings.</p>\n<h2>Laboratory Perspective</h2>\n<p>SENO provides avian molecular testing for selected targets. This article describes a biosecurity framework, not a veterinary diagnosis or a guarantee that a collection is free of disease. The current testing menu and sample requirements should be confirmed with the laboratory. Testing is most useful when it supports isolation, sanitation, and a documented management plan.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<p><strong>Can testing replace quarantine?</strong></p>\n<p>No. Testing samples a moment in time. Quarantine provides physical separation while uncertainty is managed.</p>\n<p><strong>What is the most important biosecurity practice?</strong></p>\n<p>There is no single practice. Isolation, hand hygiene, traffic control, and cleaning work together. The weakest link is often the one that is skipped under time pressure.</p>\n<p><strong>How often should equipment be cleaned?</strong></p>\n<p>Follow the manufacturer's instructions and the facility's risk plan. Shared tools and high-contact surfaces usually need more attention than low-contact storage areas.</p>\n<p><strong>Do I need records if I only keep a few birds?</strong></p>\n<p>Yes. Even a small collection benefits from a simple record of arrivals, signs, tests, and treatments. It makes pattern recognition possible.</p>\n<p><strong>Can a negative test allow a bird to skip isolation?</strong></p>\n<p>No. A negative result covers the sample and target tested. Isolation and observation remain separate safeguards.</p>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>USDA APHIS — Defend the Flock: Biosecurity. https://www.aphis.usda.gov/livestock-poultry-disease/avian/defend-the-flock</li>\n<li>CDC — Psittacosis: About. https://www.cdc.gov/psittacosis/about/index.html</li>\n<li>CDC — Bird flu: About. https://www.cdc.gov/bird-flu/about/index.html</li>\n<li>Merck Veterinary Manual — Pet birds. https://www.merckvetmanual.com/exotic-and-laboratory-animals/pet-birds</li>\n<li>WOAH — Avian influenza. https://www.woah.org/en/disease/avian-influenza/</li>\n<li>AAVLD — Accreditation requirements. https://www.aavld.org/accreditation-requirements</li>\n</ol>";
